    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "Ships have their thrust power scaled by a multiplier for lateral and reverse movement, with lateral thrust (side to side / up down) usually being the weakest. Main forward thrusters are always at 1.00x."
    },
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "Tracks are annotated by two lines: one fixed length line that indicates the heading of the track (if the track is a warship), and one variable length line with a circle on the end that indicates the track's velocity. The velocity line represents the track's predicted location after 10 seconds."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "Mechanics › Linear Acceleration",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "Ships have to accelerate to max speed, and having higher thrust makes that happen faster. [todo: enter similar equation as below with drag]"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "heading": "Mechanics › Turning",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "kind": "p",
     "text": "Ships have an angular thrust for turning and rolling. It is possible to calculate the time to turn a ship's nose 180°."
    },
    {
     "kind": "table",
     "rows": [
      [
       "Hull",
       "Inertia Factor [math]\\displaystyle{ F_y }[/math]"
      ],
      [
       "Sprinter",
       "1.59"
      ],
      [
       "Raines",
       "2.16"
      ],
      [
       "Keystone",
       "4.09"
      ],
      [
       "Vauxhall",
       "8.32"
      ],
      [
       "Axford",
       "8.72"
      ],
      [
       "Solomon",
       "17.2"
      ]
     ],
     "imgs": [
      [
       [],
